course: dictionary
lesson: 16084
title: Warfare
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Warfare is a noun. It means fighting in a war, or the way a war is fought. «The history class learned about warfare in the past.» Here it means the fighting itself, not one single battle.
explain: Look for clues around the word. In «Modern warfare uses newer weapons than the wars of long ago», the word modern tells you this is about wars today. Then the words newer weapons back up that clue.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Replace warfare with fighting in a war, and ask: does the sentence still make sense? If it does, you have it.
fact: Warfare means fighting in a war, or the way a war is fought.
fact: «Modern warfare» means the way wars are fought today.
fact: «Chemical warfare» means fighting that uses chemicals as weapons.
fact: Warfare is an uncountable noun, so we do not add an s. We say warfare, not warfares.
exercise: «The history book explains how warfare has changed over many years.» What does warfare mean here? | *Fighting in a war, and the way wars are fought | A kind of peaceful farming | Writing letters to friends | Building very tall castles
exercise: «Chemical warfare uses poisonous gases as weapons.» What is chemical warfare? | Hunting for fish in a river | A way to clean a bathroom | *Fighting that uses chemicals as weapons | A school subject about baking cakes
exercise: Which spelling is correct for this uncountable noun? | *warfare | warfared | warfaring | warfares
test: «Modern warfare is very different from the battles of long ago.» What does modern warfare mean? | Old songs sung by soldiers | *The way wars are fought today | Quiet days at school | A kind of cooking
test: Which sentence uses warfare correctly? | The warfares were very long | Two warfares were in the book | Warfare are very loud | *The book was about warfare in the past
exam: Which phrase means fighting that uses chemicals as weapons? | Modern warfare | *Chemical warfare | Friendly sports | A school trip
